BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:4th Annual NAMIWalks Sonoma County
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261017T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261017T130000
UID:260812goZ6OZr1yEia9dF5i7059Q
DESCRIPTION:**Help us show that mental health is a priority.**\n\nNAMI Son
 oma County strives to make our communities a place where everyone who  ex
 periences mental health challenges\, whether as an individual or as a fami
 ly member\, friend or caregiver\, recognizes the warning signs\, can acces
 s needed  care and support\, and knows where to ask for help if needed. H
 elp us grow awareness and build a caring community. It’s free and open t
 o all!\n\n**Here’s what to expect on October 17th:**\n\n**Get to know** 
 over 20 community mental health organizations.\n\n**Enjoy live music** fro
 m the Russian River Ramblers.\n\n**Honor** a loved one or your own journey
  at our “Why I Walk” table. \n\n**Choose** to walk or just hang out
  and enjoy! \n\n**Take** your chances on wonderful raffle prizes!\n\n**En
 joy** activities for kids and kids at heart.\n\n**Cuddle** with a PALS sup
 port dog!\n\n**Bring** your water bottle and your friendly\, leashed dog.\
 n\nFor over 40 years\, NAMI Sonoma County has provided free mental health 
 education\, support\, awareness and advocacy to our communities.  Your pr
 esence will help us to raise both funding that keeps our programs free of 
 cost to participants and creates awareness.\n\n**Register at www.namiwalks
 .org/sonomacounty.**\n\n**See you on OCtober 17th!**
LOCATION:Howarth Park\, Cypress Point Picnic Area
URL:https://namiwalks.org/sonomacounty
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:2026 Sonoma County Writers Conference
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261017T083000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261017T173000
UID:260803ZE12AP77QUSvUdFwUH7WeQ
DESCRIPTION:Registration is now open for the 2026 Sonoma County Writers Co
 nference!\n\nDear Bay Area Writers and Authors:\n\nWant to become a better
  writer\, or finally turn that idea you've been sitting on for years into 
 your first book? Trying to get noticed in a sea of indie and self-publishe
 d authors? Looking to sharpen your craft\, or just wanting to spend a day 
 with fellow creatives who get it? Whether you're holding your first blank 
 page or your tenth published book\, you're welcome here.\n\nWe invite you 
 to join us for the largest writing conference in the North Bay this fall 
 — a full day of craft\, publishing\, and marketing sessions with practic
 al tools you can start using immediately\, led by working authors and indu
 stry pros\, plus a keynote from award-winning indie poet\, historical nove
 list\, and forensic historian Gini Grossenbacher.\n\nHosted by Redwood Wri
 ters\, the largest branch of the California Writers Club — writers helpi
 ng writers\, at every stage of the page. No matter where you are in your w
 riting life\, there's a seat for you here.\n\nOUR PROMISE: By the end of t
 he day\, you'll have new skills\, new connections\, and the confidence to 
 put them to work.\n\nThis conference is open to writers and authors at any
  level\, whether a member of CWC or not.\n\nSee below for the full registr
 ation details.\n\n\\*Early bird pricing through 9/15/26 — includes conti
 nental breakfast\, snacks\, and lunch\n\n\\*Ample Free Parking.\n\nWe look
  forward to meeting you\, and helping you take your next steps forward on 
 your writing journey.\n\nSincerely\,\n\nShawn Langwell\, 2026 Sonoma Count
 y Writers Conference Chair
LOCATION:Finley Center
URL:https://redwoodwriters.org/
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Claire Wells - Violin with pianist Elizabeth Dorman
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261017T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261017T210000
UID:2607157-L291EVeEeSVEf3T-sWYg
DESCRIPTION:American violinist Claire Wells\, born in 2001\, is acclaimed 
 by audiences and the press for her expressive musicality and rich\, singin
 g quality of sound. Solo concert engagements have brought her to halls lik
 e the Wigmore Hall\, the Meyerson Symphony Center\, Bass Performance Hall\
 , Teatro Degollado\, and Konzerthaus Berlin. Wells plays on a Giovanni Bat
 tista Guadagnini\, on loan from a generous donor. Having won major prizes 
 in international competitions\, Claire Wells is the Mendelssohn-Prize 1st 
 Prize winner and Commission Prize winner at the Felix Mendelssohn Barthold
 y Competition 2021\, 2nd Prize winner at the Michael Hill International Vi
 olin Competition 2023\, and has taken major prizes at the Indianapolis Int
 ernational Competition\, Mirecourt International Competition\, Lynn Harrel
 l Competition\, among others. Claire Wells grew up in a musical family\, p
 laying both violin and piano from the age of three. She studied privately 
 with Brian Lewis\, Sandy Yamamoto\, and Emanuel Borok\, and in 2017 attend
 ed the Yehudi Menuhin School with Lutsia Ibragimova. In 2020\, Claire Well
 s studied with Mihaela Martin at the Hochschule für Musik und Tanz Köln.
  Wells has performed with major orchestras such as the Dallas Symphony Orc
 hestra\, Konzerthausorchester Berlin\, the Fort Worth Symphony Orchestra\,
  the Belgrade Philharmonic Orchestra\, the Jalisco Philharmonic\, the Shen
  Zhen Symphony Orchestra\, among others. A passionate chamber musician\, C
 laire Wells has worked alongside majoartists such as Enrico Pace\, Nobuko 
 Imai\, Frans Helmerson\, Christian Tetzlaff\, and Steven Isserlis. Wells h
 as also taken coachings from the likes of Ana Chumachenco\, Boris Kuschnir
 \, Andras Schiff and Robert Levin. Claire Wells has performed at festivals
  such as the International Holland Music Sessions\, Music@Menlo chamber mu
 sic festival\, Chamber Music Connects the World\, Gstaad Festival\, the Ve
 rbier Festival\, and others.
URL:https://creativesonoma.org/event/claire-wells-violin-with-pianist-eliz
 abeth-dorman/
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Summer Collectors Lunch Pairing at Lynmar Estate
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261017T123000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261017T143000
UID:260626npLve2dU80-o0QVf3jpyJA
DESCRIPTION:Rooted in the bounty of Quail Hill Vineyard\, Lynmar Estate’
 s Summer Collectors Lunch Pairing invites guests to experience the flavors
  of Sonoma County at their seasonal peak. The elevated four-course menu sh
 owcases ingredients harvested from Lynmar’s organically farmed gardens\,
  orchards\, and olive groves. Each course is individually plated and thoug
 htfully paired with Lynmar’s acclaimed estate-grown Pinot Noirs\, Chardo
 nnays\, and sparkling wines\, creating a journey that is both deeply conne
 cted to Sonoma County and globally inspired. Served in the idyllic setting
  of Quail Hill Vineyard\, the experience is further enhanced by a special 
 tableside visit from Estate Chef David Frakes.\n\nCollectors Lunch Pairing
  Menu Summer 2026:\n\nAdam’s Vineyard Chardonnay\, 2022\n\nWarm Organic 
 Popcorn\n\n*White Corn-Kasundi Salt\, Estate Extra-Virgin Olive Oil*\n\nSu
 sanna’s Vineyard Chardonnay\, 2022\n\nThai Green Curry & Coconut Soup\n\
 n*Bliss Snap Peas & Heirloom Radish*\n\nYoung Vines Pinot Noir\, 2021\n\nH
 eirloom Potato & Cherry Tomato ‘Croustade’\n\n*Smoked Bacon-Grain Must
 ard Dressing*\n\nSusanna’s Vineyard Pinot Noir\, 2022\n\nRoasted Syrian 
 Seven-Spiced Chicken Breast\n\n*Indonesian Corn Fritter\, Summer Vegetable
  Ragout\, Balado Sauce*\n\nRosé de Blancs Sparkling\, 2021\n\nChilled Van
 illa Burnt Basque Cheesecake\n\n*Raspberry-Rose Syrup\, Black Tea & Licori
 ce-Poached Cherries*\n\n*Brown Butter Nilla Cookie\, Candied Lime*\n\n**Es
 tate Ingredients:**\n\nBay Laurel\, Cherries\, Chives\, Edible Flowers\, L
 emons\, Olive Oil\, Peas\, Potatoes\, Radishes\, Raspberries\, Thyme\, Tom
 atoes\, Zucchini and more!\n\nMenu by Estate Chef David Frakes\n\nReservat
 ions are required. Maximum group size is six guests.
LOCATION:Lynmar Estate 
URL:https://lynmarestate.com/reservation/type/collectors-lunch-pairing/ 
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Public Garden Tour
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261017T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261017T140000
UID:260605Xazw8oNOmEqcI2eSRSwRxg
DESCRIPTION:Join us for a guided tour of the Occidental Arts & Ecology Cen
 ter's demonstration gardens and living landscapes. Led by rotating OAEC st
 aff\, these tours offer an opportunity to explore our diverse food\, medic
 inal\, pollinator\, and native plant gardens while learning about the ecol
 ogical principles that guide our work. \n\nWhen: Tours start at 1 pm ever
 y first and third Saturday of the month\, April through October. \n\nWher
 e: Tours start and end at the OAEC Nursery. 15290 Coleman Valley Rd\, Occi
 dental\, CA 95465. Upper Driveway. \n\nImportant Information:\n\nNo regis
 tration required—just show up!\n\nPlease use the second driveway and par
 k in the upper lot near the greenhouses.\n\nThe tour route includes steep 
 hills and unpaved dirt and gravel paths. Sturdy shoes\, a brimmed hat\, an
 d weather-appropriate layers are recommended.\n\nIf you require mobility s
 upport or have other accessibility needs\, please call ahead so we can acc
 ommodate you. Monday–Friday\, contact the main office at \\(707\\) 874-1
 557 ext. 101.\n\nA highlight of every tour is the OAEC Nursery\, a nonprof
 it nursery dedicated to uplifting ecologically and culturally important pl
 ants. The nursery features a wide selection of perennial food crops\, medi
 cinal and native plants\, and an ever-evolving collection of annual vegeta
 bles and herbs adapted to our region.
LOCATION:Occidental Arts and Ecology Center
URL:https://oaec.org/events/tours/
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:"Paddle The Petaluma: Turn to The River" Guided Kayak Tour
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261017T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261017T120000
UID:260514xDJKp_aXUEewvinRoDEObw
DESCRIPTION:Paddle the Petaluma: Turn to The River is a relaxed\, beginner
 -friendly guided kayak tour exploring the past\, present\, and future of P
 etaluma’s historic waterway. Launching right from downtown at The Floath
 ouse Petaluma\, you'll take part in this Sonoma County Signature Experienc
 e by spending about 90 minutes on the water in stable sit-on-top kayaks\, 
 traveling a gentle mile-and-a-half route through “Petaluma’s Longest P
 ark.”\n\nAlong the way\, you’ll raft up at docks around town\, fascina
 ting  landmarks\, and riverfront gathering spaces to hear stories about h
 ow this Gold Rush-era “saltwater highway” helped shape Petaluma—and 
 how today’s community is continuing to reinvent and reconnect with the r
 iver. This isn’t just a history tour. You’ll also learn about the peop
 le\, businesses\, artists\, nonprofits\, and river advocates helping shape
  the waterway’s future right now.\n\nEverything is included in the $75 e
 xperience: kayak\, gear\, knowledgeable local guides\, insider river stori
 es\, and post-paddle perks at nearby businesses. Finish with plenty of tim
 e for brunch\, coffee\, shopping\, or simply hanging out downtown by the w
 ater. No experience necessary.\n\nNote: The Floathouse Petaluma is a nonpr
 ofit project dedicated to “putting butts in boats and smiles on faces”
  by offering year-round easy access to the Petaluma River in and on people
 -powered water craft through rentals\, events\, classes\, lessons and summ
 er camps.
LOCATION:The Floathouse Petaluma
URL:https://www.thefloathousepetaluma.org/turn-to-the-river
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
